### What is this question really testing?

Proactiveness and attention to detail. The interviewer wants to see that you notice early warning signs others miss and act to prevent an incident.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ProactivenessTest {
    enum Type { PROACTIVE, REACTIVE }
    public static Type evaluate(boolean noticedTrend, boolean actedEarly) {
        return (noticedTrend && actedEarly) ? Type.PROACTIVE : Type.REACTIVE;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true)); // PROACTIVE
    }
}
```

### What was the faint signal?

The number of open database connections used by the transfer service at National Bank of India was slowly creeping upward, even though nothing was broken and no alarm had fired.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class FaintSignal {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(FaintSignal.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int day1 = 52, day2 = 60, day3 = 68;
        int poolMax = 100;
        log.info("Day 1: " + day1 + "/" + poolMax + " connections");
        log.info("Day 2: " + day2 + "/" + poolMax + " connections");
        log.info("Day 3: " + day3 + "/" + poolMax + " connections");
        log.info("Trend: +8/day. No alarm yet, but clearly growing.");
    }
}
```

```text
Day 1: 52/100 connections
Day 2: 60/100 connections
Day 3: 68/100 connections
Trend: +8/day. No alarm yet, but clearly growing.
```

### How did you turn the hunch into evidence?

I pulled daily readings, calculated the growth rate, and projected when the pool would exhaust.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class TrendProjection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int currentUsage = 68;
        int poolMax = 100;
        int growthPerDay = 8;
        int remaining = poolMax - currentUsage;
        int daysUntilExhaustion = remaining / growthPerDay;
        System.out.println("Current: " + currentUsage + "/" + poolMax);
        System.out.println("Growth: +" + growthPerDay + "/day");
        System.out.println("Exhaustion in: ~" + daysUntilExhaustion + " days");
        System.out.println("This turns a hunch into a dated warning.");
    }
}
```

```text
Current: 68/100
Growth: +8/day
Exhaustion in: ~4 days
This turns a hunch into a dated warning.
```

### How did you find and fix the root cause?

Dug into the code and found connections not being released on a particular error path. Fixed the leak and verified usage flattened.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class LeakFix {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(LeakFix.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Root cause: connection not released on timeout error path");
        log.info("Fix: added finally block to release connection on all paths");
        log.info("Verified: connection usage flattened at 52/100 after fix");
        log.info("No customer ever experienced an outage.");
    }
}
```

### How did you leave behind a monitor?

Added an alert on connection usage crossing eighty percent, so the next slow leak is caught automatically instead of relying on someone happening to notice.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class PoolMonitor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int threshold = 80; // percent
        System.out.println("Alert: fires when pool usage > " + threshold + "%");
        System.out.println("Effect: next leak caught automatically, not by luck");
    }
}
```

### Why is the proactive mindset a differentiator?

Reactive engineers wait for the alarm. Proactive engineers watch trends and fix issues before the alarm ever fires. The prevented incident is invisible but far more valuable.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ProactiveValue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Reactive: alarm fires -> scramble to fix -> customers affected");
        System.out.println("Proactive: trend noticed -> fixed quietly -> no customer impact");
        System.out.println("The incident that never happened is the best incident.");
    }
}
```

### What was the impact?

Fixed the leak well before the projected exhaustion date. No customer ever experienced the outage that would otherwise have been inevitable.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class EarlyDetectionResult {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Projected outage: 4 days away");
        System.out.println("Fixed: 4 days before projected outage");
        System.out.println("Customer impact: zero");
        System.out.println("Monitor added: next leak auto-detected");
    }
}
```

